[{:db/id 17592186059584 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n![](images/46/464ba24026d89efb3e62f7807426e5c413f277da3c8087931d2e3fde9a0b8e74.svg)\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n![](images/ba/baf05a693deacc3ef644165bae4de7b1435a90c73f050ac76c48892b2e010393.svg)\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=1$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+2(1)=0 \n\t\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\n\tx=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-2$ and $y=1.$ Or, in vector form, $\\left<  -2,1 \\right>.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059776 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-13eff08d-2b22-5094-8876-818462babb6f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059585 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n![](images/7b/7b1adc08164fdcf1a5322b4fbfd3e5b0f57e22e018aa801add4bb3b572c29f76.svg)\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n![](images/87/8773defc4db521e846b58b7c5ef04b54f26723d94099c75caffbeca0587c33dc.svg)\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n![](images/45/455dc49a531a371ea8aecd6259a677c8006bb2a23181ed66d27721135f240c89.svg)\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=-10$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-5(-10)=-5 \n\t\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\n\tx=-55.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-55$ and $y=-10.$ Or, in vector form, $\\langle -55,-10 \\rangle.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-13eff08d-2b22-5094-8876-818462babb6f" :question/id #uuid "13eff08d-2b22-5094-8876-818462babb6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73554" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2x-8y=10 \\\\  & 3x-7y=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -8 & 10 \\\\ 3 & -7 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -8 & 10 \\\\ 3 & -7 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cae93ed5-7aca-5036-ae81-d4a677001210"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cae93ed5-7aca-5036-ae81-d4a677001210" :answer-field/id #uuid "cae93ed5-7aca-5036-ae81-d4a677001210" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-35507ba3-dc5e-5f36-bb3c-2cadb07fb8ff" :answer-field/choices ["answer-35507ba3-dc5e-5f36-bb3c-2cadb07fb8ff" "answer-7bad20ef-df3c-52d4-a10d-b7e2a38262ad" "answer-92cfbdd1-6bb8-5b06-89f2-6d7a9c5e7075" "answer-632206dd-bb16-5704-9288-1e7bf08e0cfc" "answer-29b00cee-94fc-5ef4-95de-2345a02742de"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35507ba3-dc5e-5f36-bb3c-2cadb07fb8ff" :answer/id #uuid "35507ba3-dc5e-5f36-bb3c-2cadb07fb8ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7bad20ef-df3c-52d4-a10d-b7e2a38262ad" :answer/id #uuid "7bad20ef-df3c-52d4-a10d-b7e2a38262ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-92cfbdd1-6bb8-5b06-89f2-6d7a9c5e7075" :answer/id #uuid "92cfbdd1-6bb8-5b06-89f2-6d7a9c5e7075" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-632206dd-bb16-5704-9288-1e7bf08e0cfc" :answer/id #uuid "632206dd-bb16-5704-9288-1e7bf08e0cfc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29b00cee-94fc-5ef4-95de-2345a02742de" :answer/id #uuid "29b00cee-94fc-5ef4-95de-2345a02742de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060941 :tutorial/content "Consider the following system of linear equations and its respective **augmented matrix**:\n![](images/2c/2c5dfe046037750f98f773ad906094f5ffbad3c285adf200134a119f72888ac8.svg)\n\nIf we can convert the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form, then we can solve the system using back substitution.\n\nThe procedure for reducing $M$ to row echelon form using elementary row operations is called **Gaussian elimination**. To perform Gaussian elimination on a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, we use the following procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** Consider the entry $a_{11}=2$ of the coefficient matrix. This entry is called a **pivot element**. We want to make this entry equal to $1$. So, the first elementary row operation will be \n\n$$\nR_1 := \\dfrac{1}{2}R_1\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Consider the entry $a_{21}=3$ below the first pivot element. We want to make this entry equal to $0$. So, the second elementary row operation will be \n\n$$\nR_2 :=R_2+(-3)R_1\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, consider the entry $a_{22}=-10$. This entry is the second pivot element, and we would like to make it equal to $1$. So, the third elementary row operation will be \n\n$$\nR_2 :=\\dfrac{1}{-10}R_2\n$$\n\nand we get $M$ in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{-10}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n![](images/b8/b83c4646349bada803311e5e9e160aafc8f08e6f51c7b69302def5ca4eeaa3fa.svg)\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+3 \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) = -1 \n\t\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\tx=\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ Or, in vector form, $\\left< \\dfrac{1}{2}, -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right>.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060942 :tutorial/content "The row echelon form of a matrix is *not* unique. In the previous example, after the first row operation we arrived at \n\n![](images/eb/ebcb9bc5aedf609284436ad1c10e98f9b22d91a382136b5aabfb3f18c736ddae.svg)\n\nThis augmented matrix is already in row echelon form! However, it is always useful to do an extra step and obtain $1$'s along the main diagonal, like we did previously:\n\n![](images/53/536245bdc7fc9dab02723d3576adff774dfd9711551f472333c027d83f76c05b.svg)\n\nHaving $1$'s along the main diagonal makes the final back substitution process a little easier." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059884 :activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
